WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.740
Why would a glial cell wrap an axon in dozens of layers of membrane?

2
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.300
And why are some axons never wrapped?

3
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.970
Start with one patch of membrane.

4
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.190
Current spreads to it from the active stretch behind.

5
00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.450
Before it can fire, that current must charge it.

6
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:23.693
In a bare axon, current also leaks out at every point.

7
00:00:24.493 --> 00:00:26.513
So the signal is rebuilt on every patch.

8
00:00:27.513 --> 00:00:29.473
Less current is left to charge the patch ahead.

9
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:35.367
Myelin is glial membrane, wound many times around the axon and squeezed tight.

10
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.100
Each turn adds two membranes in series.

11
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:41.400
So the wall's capacitance falls.

12
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.660
And its resistance rises.

13
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.100
Far more of the current now reaches the next gap.

14
00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:50.123
Those gaps are the nodes of Ranvier.

15
00:00:51.063 --> 00:00:53.223
The axon packs its sodium channels there.

16
00:00:54.166 --> 00:00:57.326
The signal is rebuilt at the nodes, so it seems to jump.

17
00:00:58.166 --> 00:00:59.566
This is saltatory conduction.

18
00:01:03.999 --> 00:01:07.759
In a bare axon, speed rises with the square root of diameter.

19
00:01:08.699 --> 00:01:12.259
In a myelinated fiber, it rises in proportion to diameter.

20
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:17.083
So which axon gains more from a wrap, a thin one or a thick one?

21
00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:28.193
The thick one. In a simple model, the gain grows with the axon's size.

22
00:01:29.266 --> 00:01:31.946
The axon diameter is the axon alone.

23
00:01:32.746 --> 00:01:34.686
The fiber diameter adds the myelin.

24
00:01:35.699 --> 00:01:37.679
Their ratio is the g ratio.

25
00:01:38.519 --> 00:01:41.799
A thinner sheath on the same axon gives a higher g ratio.

26
00:01:42.832 --> 00:01:47.312
Healthy peripheral fibers sit near 0.6 to 0.7.

27
00:01:48.192 --> 00:01:51.452
That is close to the fastest value, but the optimum is broad.

28
00:01:52.482 --> 00:01:58.142
Now the puzzle. In peripheral nerve, axons up to about 1 micrometer stay bare.

29
00:01:59.002 --> 00:02:03.762
Some cable models of small central fibers put the break-even for myelin much lower,

30
00:02:04.222 --> 00:02:06.502
so speed alone may not explain the cutoff.

31
00:02:07.616 --> 00:02:10.056
Space and energy cost may matter too.

32
00:02:10.976 --> 00:02:12.356
Something in the tissue may decide.

33
00:02:16.816 --> 00:02:18.356
Here is the idea to carry.

34
00:02:19.196 --> 00:02:24.336
The speed gain is largest on large axons, and nerves do match the wrap to the axon.

35
00:02:27.349 --> 00:02:29.049
In nerve, the axon decides.

36
00:02:30.029 --> 00:02:34.369
In the brain and spinal cord, the oligodendrocyte brings much of its own program.
